Based on the information shared:

* The email from Box was legitimate and sent by the People Team for employment-related purposes.
* The shared links were previously configured without mandatory authentication, allowing access to anyone with the link.
** The configuration issue has since been corrected — access to shared files on Box requires authentication via Box login.
* Links already had expiration dates, and emails were sent only to validated addresses from Workday (*minimising likelihood of exposure*).
* No evidence of unauthorized access or data compromise has been identified to date.
* The likelihood of any third party having accessed the documents is *_very_* *_low_*, given the limited exposure path (links only in individual emails, not indexed or public). The only scenario in which a 3rd party could view the documents would be if the sender/recipient themselves voluntarily shared the link with someone.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Conclusion:*
This was a _low-risk configuration issue_ rather than an actual personal data breach. Mitigations have been implemented, and no further action is required at this stage.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
*Follow-up:*

* System owner (People Team) should document this configuration change internally for audit purposes.
* I recommend minor awareness raising with the People Team to ensure proper use of access control settings in future document sharing workflows.
{panel}

