Thanks [~accountid:712020:b2a2738f-c1f1-447c-9b72-29851b5fed3e] - I agree we should align internally before responding. My thoughts on this are below.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
# *_Are we able to word comms in such a manner that would still allow us to communicate over email & push (e.g. not naming offer providers directly, talking about it more as an app feature or part of the Bolt Rewards loyalty program ) to those with only promotional consent, but not specifically third-party consent ?_*
{panel}

* I highly doubt there is a way that we could word the comms to frame them as service / administrative messages (i.e., non-marketing).Ultimately, the things we are pushing here are part of a third-party loyalty rewards program, and so a purposive approach would always lead to the conclusion that the comms are promotional. This is particularly likely in Poland, Romania and France, which are all high risk countries regarding marketing comms.
* Not naming the specific offer providers does not change anything here. We would still be promoting Bolt Rewards, and so the comms would still count as marketing.
* {color:#4c9aff}*Conclusion*{color}: IMO this is always going to be interpreted as marketing. If we want to get around the consent issue, our focus should be on promoting Bot Rewards through less _direct_ means (i.e., more creative uses of general advertising, in-time requests for consent to marketing comms, etc.).

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
# *_if the answer is no to the first question, can we use in-app channels such as pop-up modals and app inbox for the awareness comms?_*
{panel}

* Displaying in-app banners in inboxes (i.e., a similar format to emails) would likely still count as ‘direct’ marketing.
* Pop-up modals could be something to explore, as with in-app banners. We could also consider asking for ‘in-time’ consent (i.e., pop-ups asking for marketing consent when drivers visit the Bolt Rewards page) as a means of getting more users to consent to third-party marketing.
* Push notifications would still be ‘direct’ marketing, but they are lower risk than emails. As such, if the business is desperate, we could explore these as well.
* {color:#4c9aff}*Conclusion*{color}: Without third-party consent, we could use the following marketing mediums - (a) banners; (b) pop-up modals; and (if strictly needed) (c) push notifications. We can also deploy ‘in-time’ consent requests.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
# *_further down the line, we'd also like to communicate individual savings (e.g. you've saved X this month with Bolt Rewards / Offers) - would this be classified as a transactional comm if we only send this to drivers based on their activity? Or promotional? Or third-party marketing consent would still apply?_*
{panel}

* On balance, particularly in higher-risk countries, this would still be interpreted as a promotional communication - the bottom line is that the purpose of the email is to promote Bolt Rewards. We could of course try and frame it in such a way as to present it as transactional, but if a regulator became interested I don’t think it would do us much good.
* Nevertheless, admittedly, these comms would be lower risk than those directly encouraging drivers to sign up for Bolt Rewards (given that these ‘savings’ emails would be sent to drivers who are already benefitting from Bolt Rewards, and so they are less likely to complain).
* {color:#4c9aff}*Conclusion*{color}: These would still be marketing comms requiring consent. However, the risk profile is lower here, as drivers are unlikely to complain. As such, we could consider eating the risk if the business is desperate (although it is not something we should recommend in the first instance).

