Info from [~accountid:607a94ce95f6fc006ad02d1b] :

In the code, it extensively use Date.now(). Most notably here:
[https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/develop/common/workflows/cron/WorkflowRunnerCron.ts#L69|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/develop/common/workflows/cron/WorkflowRunnerCron.ts#L69|smart-link] 

{noformat}            await this.workflowsRepo.setLockedUntil(runCtx, {
                locked_until: new Date(Date.now()),
                runIds: [workflow.run_id]
            });{noformat}

so instead of

{noformat}UPDATE workflow SET locked_until = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(){noformat}

it will pass the timestamp from JS

{noformat}UPDATE workflow SET locked_until = ?{noformat}

As in JS timers might be not in sync with MySQL (and also, I guess, the rounding is different), this specific code results (sometimes) to setting locked_until in the future, which in turn makes tests flaky.
The suggestion is drop Date.now() in updates, use MySQL time.
Same actually applies here: [https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/develop/common/workflows/runningCtrl.ts#L319|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/develop/common/workflows/runningCtrl.ts#L319] 
Would be more consistent to use DB time there or leave some injection point to guarantee tests stability

